  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* ORTHOGONALITY. The determinant order is read off numerically (log-log slope over eps = 10^-2, 10^-3, 10^-4): the leading order is high, so a symbolic Series scan would need a deeper truncation than the entries support. The point is how each order responds to the truncation Ntr. NORMS sit at a FIXED order (14 for A, 16 for V) whatever Ntr is. CROSS terms track Ntr + 12 exactly, i.e. they vanish to every order the expansion resolves and what remains is pure truncation artifact. That is orthogonality holding order by order in the twist. *)\nClearAll[ordNumD];\nordNumD[M_] := Round[-Differences[Log[10, Table[Abs[Det[N[M /. eps -> e, 60]]], {e, {1/100, 1/1000, 1/10000}}]]], 0.01];\nGrid[Prepend[Table[{tr, ordNumD[spMat[1, 1, dulQ[rAd12, {2, 1, 1, 0}, 12, tr], dirQ[rA12, 1, 1, {2, 1, 1, 0}, 12, tr], tr]][[1]], ordNumD[spMat[1, 1, dulQ[rVd12, {2, 2, 0, 0}, 12, tr], dirQ[rV12, 1, 1, {2, 2, 0, 0}, 12, tr], tr]][[1]], ordNumD[spMat[1, 1, dulQ[rAd12, {2, 1, 1, 0}, 12, tr], dirQ[rV12, 1, 1, {2, 2, 0, 0}, 12, tr], tr]][[1]], ordNumD[spMat[1, 1, dulQ[rVd12, {2, 2, 0, 0}, 12, tr], dirQ[rA12, 1, 1, {2, 1, 1, 0}, 12, tr], tr]][[1]]}, {tr, {5, 6, 7, 8, 10}}], {\"truncation Ntr\", \"det A|A (norm)\", \"det V|V (norm)\", \"det A|V (cross)\", \"det V|A (cross)\"}], Frame -> All]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
