  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### d=1: an untwisted Q-function formula, proved for the six tested spin pairs\n\nThe regular direct limit has P1=1 and P2(u)=u^2+(h-2r0)u, up to adding a constant. Indeed,\n\n`-2 [Q2-(h z1/(z1-z2)-r) Q1]/(phi1-phi2) -> P2`\n\nas all twists vanish along a generic ray. Therefore the monic Casoratian is exactly `Qbar12(u)=u-r0`, where `r0=(SP h+P theta1+S theta2)/(S+P)`. Its top values satisfy\n\n`Qbar12(theta1+S h) = h S(S+t)/(S+P)` and `Qbar12(theta2+P h) = h P(P-t)/(S+P)`.\n\nThe exact on-shell rational determinants give, with symbolic t, for (S,P)=(1,1),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1),\n\n$$\\frac{N_{d=1}}{N_{\\mathrm{vac}}}=\\frac{(SP)^3 h^6}{(S+P)^5}[(S+t)(P-t)]^3=(S+P)\\left[\\bar Q_{12}(\\theta_1+Sh)\\bar Q_{12}(\\theta_2+Ph)\\right]^3.$$\n\nThe displayed h dependence follows by scaling u=h v and the monic Q polynomials. At h=1 the constants are respectively 1/32, 8/243, 1/16, 27/1024, 216/3125, 64/3125. This is an exact result for those six spin pairs, and a conjecture for arbitrary positive integer S,P. The vacuum formula and exact twisted d=1 Q-functions, by contrast, have proofs for general S,P.\n\nThe d=1 identity is possible because its Casoratian is linear; it does not solve the d>=2 cases or invalidate the earlier failed product searches across all states. The same degree d need not uniquely label an irrep when both spins exceed 1, so further constants should be indexed by mu until dependence on d alone is proved.\n\n**Verification:** all four symbolic Baxter residuals vanish modulo the quadratic root equation; direct and dual Laurent coefficients match the saved perturbative solutions through eps^8; an independent rational ray gives the same d=1 coefficient at (2,2); the finite-twist vacuum identity passes exact tests including h=2/3.\n\n**Next:** prove the d=1 norm identity for arbitrary spins from the on-shell finite determinant; then extend the exact method to mu={L-1,L-1,1,1} (d=2). The on-shell substitution is essential: set r=r0+eps, delta=-(S+P) eps/[(r-theta1)(r-theta2)] at h=1, and z=1+delta {3,0,1,-3}. It removes the square root before taking Det and makes the symbolic-separation checks finish in seconds. Retaining eps, r and t as three independent variables timed out.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Session 4: d>=2\n\nStart with mu={L-1,L-1,1,1}, L=S+P (d=2), where both direct and both dual branch polynomials are monic and linear. Keep the exact finite-twist Baxter equations and eliminate transfer coefficients before seeking a rational on-shell parametrisation.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "ClearAll[d2b, d2eq, d2tc1, d2eq2, d2mu];\nd2mu = {exS + exP - 1, exS + exP - 1, 1, 1};\nd2eq = Table[DeleteCases[CoefficientList[Cancel[BaxRes[exS, exP, d2mu][kk][Function[y, y + d2b[kk]]][x]/(ν1[exS, exP][x - 2 h] ν1[exS, exP][x - 3 h])], x], 0], {kk, 2}];\nd2tc1 = First[Quiet[Solve[Thread[d2eq[[1]] == 0], tcvars], Solve::svars]];\nd2eq2 = DeleteCases[Factor /@ (d2eq[[2]] /. d2tc1), 0];\n{Length[d2tc1], Length[d2eq2], Factor[Last[d2eq2]]}",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
